Problems Serving Routed React App

Everyone’s out here creating react apps using create-react-app and it makes getting started very easy. I find that whenever I try to add some backend functionality, things usually break.

I could complain about whole bunch of things about create-react-app but for now, let’s just solve one problem I recently ran into.

Context

I have a bunch of routes on my SPA and some API’s in my Express application. I’m serving up the UI using Express’s static asset rendering middleware: app.use(express.static(path.join(__dirname, 'public')) . And this is going great… Until I try to directly access the UI with a non-empty path. Here I get the message cannot GET /path .

Solution: Express-Sessions

Here is the whole file but we will break it down step-by-step:

Import Dependencies

import express from 'express';

import * as path from 'path;

import cookieParser from 'cookie-parser';

import session from 'express-session';



import apiRoutes from './api';

cookie-parser : Cookie parser is needed to support sessions

: Cookie parser is needed to support sessions express-session : Will hold temporary information while redirecting to and from the UI in our server.

: Will hold temporary information while redirecting to and from the UI in our server. ./api : This is just a placeholder for all of your server routes.

Creating Server and Applying Middleware

const app = express();

app.use(cookieParser());

app.use(session({

secret: 'my super secret secret',

resave: false,

saveUninitialized: true,

});

Add Server Routes

// Put your non-UI routes here, i.e. /api.

app.use('/api', apiRoutes);

Here, /api is just a placeholder for any prefix you want. In fact, you don’t even need to provide a prefix for this to work, I just personally like to explicitly separate my API’s.

Serve Built Static Assets

if (process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production') {

const publicPath = path.join(__dirname, 'public');

// Serve up built frontend application as static assets

app.use(express.static(path.join(publicPath)));

The Interesting Part

The interesting thing to note here is that the static asset serving is just helping express redirect requests for static assets at / to serve files designated in path.join(...) . The issue arises when a web browser requests a UI path that doesn’t get mapped to this static asset serving middleware, for instance /page1 .

The naive solution would just be to do something like:

app.get('*', (req, res) => res.sendFile(..., 'index.html'));

However, this doesn’t actually render anything! That’s because each subsequent request for static assets (the files loaded by index.html ) are automatically prepended with the /page1 prefix. For instance, if there is a stylesheet index.css defined in your index.html file, the request for this will actually be a GET /page1/index.css instead of just /index.css .

To handle this, we want to first send index.html , and then tell the webapp to redirect to the initial path. We use express-session to store the requested unmatched path.

To bind this to GraphQL we will need 2 things, Firstly a Schema, Secondly Resolvers.

The Schema describes what is provided by our Resolvers, it describes our API, the Resolvers dispatch requests to our application code.

Under src/graphql add the resolvers.ts

import { PubSub } from "apollo-server-express";

import { addLikes, getLikes } from "../actions";



const likes = new PubSub();



export const resolvers = {

RootQuery: {

getLikes: () => getLikes(),

},

RootMutation: {

addLikes: async (_, { count }) => {

const newLikeCount = addLikes(count);

await likes.publish("NEW_LIKE", { likes: newLikeCount });

return newLikeCount;

},

},

RootSubscription: {

likes: {

subscribe: () => likes.asyncIterator(["NEW_LIKE"]),

},

},

};

Under src/graphql add the schema.ts

import { gql } from "apollo-server-express";



export const typeDefs = gql`

schema {

query: RootQuery

mutation: RootMutation

subscription: RootSubscription

}



type RootQuery {

"""

Get the current number of likes

"""

getLikes: Int

}



type RootMutation {

"""

Add a new like

"""

addLikes(count: Int): Int

}



type RootSubscription {

"""

Subscribe to new Like events as they occur

"""

likes: Int

}

`;

You'll notice there is a 1–2–1 relationship between the Schema and the Resolvers, this is necessary otherwise the application will fail to load.

When visiting http://localhost:3000/graphql you’ll get the GraphQL playground, from here you can browse the schema and write queries/mutations and subscriptions.

Each time you run an action it gets sent to our server and GraphQL will see it, and hand off to the associated Resolver matching by name and signature.

Queries are very simple and self explanatory.

Mutations need to be async and inside function we deconstruct the second parameter matching names as we described in our schema. We can see in our example while calling our internal code to increment the likes we also call likes.publish("NEW_LIKE", {likes: newLikeCount}) in this block we effectively push to the likes PubSub instance a new event. This is what our Subscription then watches and informs anyone subscribed that there is a new message.

